      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "Since the center of the circle is on the $x$-axis, we can represent its coordinates as $(a, 0)$. To find the radius, we calculate the distance between the point $(0, 4)$ and the center of the circle, $\\sqrt{(0 - a)^2 + (4 - 0)^2}$. \n\nGiven that the circle passes through the point $(1, 3)$, we can also express the distance between $(1, 3)$ and the center of the circle as $\\sqrt{(1 - a)^2 + (3 - 0)^2}$. \n\nWe equate these two expressions because they both represent the radius, using the given points on the circle.\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(0 - a)^2 + (4 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{(1 - a)^2 + (3 - 0)^2}\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\n\\[\n(0 - a)^2 + 4^2 = (1 - a)^2 + 3^2\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides:\n\n\\[\na^2 + 16 = 1 - 2a + a^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation by removing $a^2$ and combining like terms:\n\n\\[\n16 = -2a + 10\n\\]\n\nSolving for $a$:\n\n\\[\n2a = -6\n\\]\n\\[\na = -3\n\\]\n\nNow, the center of the circle is at $(-3, 0)$. Using this information, we can find the radius by calculating the distance between $(0, 4)$ and $(-3, 0)$:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Radius} = \\sqrt{(0 - (-3))^2 + (4 - 0)^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Radius} = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Radius} = \\sqrt{9 + 16}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Radius} = \\sqrt{25}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Radius} = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the inscribed circle is $\\boxed{5}$.",
