      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the radius of a circle that passes through the points \\((0,4)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) and whose center is on the \\(x\\)-axis.\n\nFirst, let's denote the center of the circle as \\((h, 0)\\). The distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to each of the points \\((0,4)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) must be equal to the radius \\(r\\) of the circle. Therefore, we can write the following equations based on the distance formula:\n\n1. Distance from \\((h, 0)\\) to \\((0, 4)\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\n\n2. Distance from \\((h, 0)\\) to \\((1, 3)\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to the radius \\(r\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nNext, we square both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nExpand the right-hand side:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\(h^2\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\n\nIsolate \\(h\\):\n\\[\n16 - 10 = -2h\n\\]\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(h = -3\\), we can find the radius \\(r\\) using the distance formula from the center \\((-3, 0)\\) to one of the points, say \\((0, 4)\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(-3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
